  "assumptions": [
    "Illustration: a made-up two-setting loss bowl with simulated batch noise, not a real model trained on real data.",
    "Loss = (2.4 x² + 0.6 z²)/2, drawn at 0.4 × loss height. x (left-right) is the steep direction. Without noise, x swings grow once the learning rate is above 2/2.4 ≈ 0.83.",
    "Each step moves by -rate × (true slope + noise). The noise on each slope component is Gaussian with sd √((64-b)/(63b)), so it is 1 at batch 1 and exactly 0 at 64. Noise comes from a fixed seed, and each new run uses the next values.",
    "Both arrows are steps (rate × slope), drawn at 3 times their true length with the same scale. Mini-batch step is the ball's actual next move. Full-data step is the step the true slope would give. Hidden when the run ends.",
    "Status: running; finished after 40 steps; diverged when the loss goes above 5 or the ball leaves the drawn bowl. Points off the bowl are drawn at its rim, and the readout keeps the true loss.",
    "The run takes 5 steps per second at 1× speed. The trail marks every step's landing point, up to 40. The ghost keeps the previous run's path.",
    "With noise, a single step can raise the loss. It goes down only on average."
  ]
}